## Vercel Web Analytics Installation

Successfully installed and configured Vercel Web Analytics for the Nepal Agriculture Dashboard project.

### Changes Made

**1. Installed @vercel/analytics package (v2.0.1)**
   - Added dependency to package.json using pnpm
   - Updated pnpm-lock.yaml with new dependency tree

**2. Modified src/App.tsx**
   - Added import: `import { Analytics } from "@vercel/analytics/react";`
   - Placed `<Analytics />` component at the end of the BrowserRouter component
   - Follows official Vercel documentation for React/Vite projects

### Implementation Details

The implementation follows the official Vercel Analytics quickstart guide for React/Vite applications:
- Fetched the latest documentation from https://vercel.com/docs/analytics/quickstart
- Used framework-specific instructions for React/Vite projects
- Added the Analytics component in the root App component to track all page views
- Component placement ensures analytics are tracked across all routes

### Verification Steps Completed

✅ Type checking passed (tsc --noEmit)
✅ Build completed successfully (pnpm run build)
✅ Linter passed with 0 warnings (pnpm run lint)
✅ All 30 tests passed (pnpm run test:run)

### Next Steps for Deployment

To complete the setup:
1. Deploy the application to Vercel
2. Enable Web Analytics in the Vercel dashboard (Project Settings > Analytics)
3. After deployment, verify analytics tracking by checking for XHR requests to `/_vercel/insights/*`
4. Analytics data will appear in the Vercel dashboard within a few days of user traffic

### Files Modified

- frontend/package.json - Added @vercel/analytics dependency
- frontend/pnpm-lock.yaml - Updated lockfile with new dependencies
- frontend/src/App.tsx - Added Analytics component import and usage

The implementation is production-ready and follows Vercel's recommended best practices for React/Vite applications.
